# Review package: ticket 07, 77fd93e..bb355d0

## Commits
### bb355d0
perf(state): dispatch direct push and pop from the cached wrappers

A probe with the base's exact getWrappedArrayMethod body read plain
objects at the base's speed, while every other body shape measured 2
to 5% slower, even where the added code never runs for a plain
object. Restore that body unchanged and move the direct dispatch into
the wrappers the cache hands out for the native push and pop: they
find the array's raw target and composed hooks through the Proxy
receiver in directArrayContexts, which state() fills for array
targets. A receiver without an entry gets the native method in a
batch, as before. This drops the per-target wrapper pair, the second
guard and the lookup helper (Q61 a).

### 7f839b3
perf(state): keep the three-argument guard call on hookless reads

The 100 states individual benchmark ran about 6% slower than the base
over four interleaved series. The only change on its path is the call
every property read makes to the array method guard, which gained a
fourth argument for the hooks. Give the hookless trap a guard with the
three-argument shape it called before, and the hooked trap its own
four-argument guard (Q61 a).

### ae22ed0
fix(state): leave a hole for push(undefined) on the direct path

The set trap skips an equal value without writing, so push(undefined)
through the Proxy path never created the index and only grew length.
The direct path ran the native push first and stored an own element.
Delete the skipped slot again so the array ends as it did before
(Q62 b), and pin that with the hook sequence and the wakes on both
kinds of state. Also compare the hook sequence from inside a child
effect to the plain Proxy reference, and list the new test file in
the tests index.

### b41d79f
perf(state): leave only the two cheap checks on the property read path

The plain-object read benchmark still ran about 4% slower with the
push/pop identity check inside getWrappedArrayMethod, which every
property read calls. Keep only Array.isArray and the typeof check
there and move the identity check, the direct pair lookup, the cache
hit and the name check into lookupArrayMethod.

### 0ab02f3
perf(state): keep the array method lookup small on the read path

Every property read calls getWrappedArrayMethod. With the direct
push/pop lookup inlined into it, the plain-object read benchmark ran
about 4% slower across three interleaved runs. Move that lookup into
getDirectArrayMethod so the body on the read path stays as small as
before.

### 21bfffd
perf(state): take a direct path for push and pop on reactive arrays

About half of the array push/pop benchmark's self time went to V8's
generic push and pop on a Proxy receiver. Run the native method on the
raw target instead and replay by hand what the get, set and delete
traps would have done: read length (and the last index for pop), set
each new index and length, delete the popped index. The replay calls
onRead, onWrite and onDelete with the arguments a direct property
access produces, runs the loop check before the equal skip inside an
effect, schedules per property inside an effect and marks the array
dirty once outside one, so no observable behavior changes. The other
five mutating methods keep the Proxy receiver; a push or pop called on
another receiver falls back to it as well.

Pin the hook call sequence and the woken subscribers for both kinds of
state against a plain Proxy over the same array as the reference.


